{"text": " It's a country that solved its debt problem by buying its own debt. They're trapped by a financial monster of their own creation. To hold borrowing costs at zero for that long, the Bank of Japan had to buy its own government's bonds, trillions of yen of them, year after year, until it owned about half of the entire Japanese government bond market. And those bonds pay almost nothing, because paying nothing was the entire point. So investors dump the yen, chase the higher yield in dollars, and drive the currency off a cliff. Which is usually where the armchair economists show up and ask, why doesn't Japan just raise interest rates and close the gap? Problem solved, and it's because they can't. But the cash commercial banks park at the Bank of Japan get paid whatever the central bank's rate is. So the moment the Bank of Japan raises rates to close the gap and save the yen, it starts paying out trillions of yen in interest while still collecting almost nothing on the pile it's stuck holding. You don't need to have a degree in central banking to know that's a horrible deal. So Japan can now either defend the yen or it can afford the debt. It can't do both.", "segments": [{"id": 0, "seek": 0, "start": 0.0, "end": 3.36, "text": " It's a country that solved its debt problem by buying its own debt.", "tokens": [50363, 632, 338, 257, 1499, 326, 16019, 663, 5057, 1917, 416, 7067, 663, 898, 5057, 13, 50543], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11480582632669588, "compression_ratio": 1.697749196141479, "no_speech_prob": 0.0005860374658368528, "words": [{"word": " It's", "start": 0.0, "end": 0.3, "probability": 0.7823362648487091}, {"word": " a", "start": 0.3, "end": 0.38, "probability": 0.9916257262229919}, {"word": " country", "start": 0.38, "end": 0.56, "probability": 0.9927491545677185}, {"word": " that", "start": 0.56, "end": 0.82, "probability": 0.9954116940498352}, {"word": " solved", "start": 0.82, "end": 1.06, "probability": 0.9770986437797546}, {"word": " i